, 0.0206756591796875, 0.04119873046875, -0.0093231201171875, 0.0237579345703125, 0.006633758544921875, 0.022125244140625, -0.01116943359375, -0.0256805419921875, 0.0253448486328125, 0.0262298583984375, 0.02178955078125, -0.044342041015625, 0.00878143310546875, -0.0621337890625, 2.6881694793701172e-05, -0.033660888671875, -0.0011873245239257812, -0.025238037109375, -0.040191650390625, -0.0025177001953125, 0.0151214599609375, -0.007129669189453125, 0.0041351318359375, 0.0295257568359375, 0.0080718994140625, -0.007843017578125, -0.0338134765625, -0.008392333984375, 0.005268096923828125, -0.004962921142578125, -0.035980224609375, -0.0305938720703125, -0.0068511962890625, 0.047515869140625, -0.006923675537109375, -0.016357421875, 0.0273284912109375, 0.0362548828125, 0.044647216796875, 0.0271453857421875, 0.00466156005859375, -0.040740966796875, 0.02459716796875, 0.015045166015625, -0.00595855712890625, 0.0013132095336914062, 0.017913818359375, 0.0275421142578125, -0.00786590576171875, 0.00861358642578125, -0.0020198822021484375, -0.0305938720703125, -0.002899169921875, 0.0026397705078125, 0.00568389892578125, 0.0008492469787597656, -0.01415252685546875, 0.002605438232421875, -0.0265655517578125, -0.015899658203125, 0.01348114013671875, -0.0175323486328125, 0.01082611083984375, 0.0051422119140625, -0.0085601806640625, 0.046875, -0.033233642578125, 0.01245880126953125, -0.0180816650390625, 0.02740478515625, -0.03424072265625, 0.0311279296875, 0.0210418701171875, 0.028564453125, -0.00463104248046875, -0.01264190673828125, 0.0460205078125, 0.003177642822265625, -0.0215911865234375, 0.0178375244140625, 0.019287109375, -0.01470947265625, 0.059722900390625, 0.0145721435546875, 0.010162353515625, -0.0026111602783203125, -2.0503997802734375e-05, -0.0028057098388671875, 0.02423095703125, -0.007350921630859375, 0.01715087890625, -0.004253387451171875, 0.00750732421875, -0.013153076171875, 0.028564453125, 0.05413818359375, 0.0594482421875, -0.0250396728515625, 0.0005993843078613281, 0.00605010986328125, 0.0272674560546875, -0.026123046875, -0.01053619384765625, 0.0288238525390625, -0.006206512451171875, 0.0133819580078125, -0.0099029541015625, 0.02899169921875, 0.02777099609375, 0.0347900390625, 0.0216217041015625, -0.004444122314453125, -0.00984954833984375, 0.0146636962890625, 0.03558349609375, -0.03021240234375, -0.0014200210571289062, -0.04193115234375, -0.0435791015625, 0.019622802734375, -0.014495849609375, 0.00811004638671875, -0.006496429443359375, 0.006298065185546875, -0.004638671875, 0.0094146728515625, -0.0116119384765625, -0.03076171875, -0.0175018310546875, -0.002620697021484375, 0.00341796875, 0.01169586181640625, 0.0003952980041503906, 0.005741119384765625, -0.002925872802734375, 0.0194244384765625, 0.0006551742553710938, 0.006633758544921875, 0.021240234375, 0.0018930435180664062, 0.0328369140625, -0.02008056640625, -0.005107879638671875, 0.0099945068359375, -0.008209228515625, -0.0067596435546875, -0.001552581787109375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\n1. **Introduction and Motivation**  \nCategorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While AI is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, in-house small language models (SLMs) offer potential advantages in cost, data security, confidentiality, and interpretability. In this work, we focus on SLMs\u2014specifically SBERT and DeBERTa\u2014due to their strong performance in sentence-level tasks, efficient resource requirements, and widespread adoption in industry for document classification. Other SLMs were considered, but SBERT and DeBERTa were selected based on their proven empirical results, resource efficiency, and availability of robust pre-trained weights. Models such as DistilBERT and ALBERT were excluded after preliminary experiments showed lower accuracy and less stable training on our dataset, and because their omission does not materially affect the generalisability of our findings for organisations with similar resource constraints.\n\n2. **Research Questions and Unique Contributions**  \nThis study addresses the following research questions, each linked to a unique contribution:  \n- **RQ1:** Can in-house SLMs, specifically SBERT and DeBERTa variants, achieve high accuracy in invoice categorisation compared to zero-shot large language models (LLMs) and vendor-based baselines?  \n  - **Contribution 1:** We provide a systematic evaluation of SLMs for invoice categorisation, including generalisation to new clients and challenging categories, and compare their performance to strong baselines.\n- **RQ2:** How does the geometry of pre-trained sentence embeddings relate to classification performance in this financial context, particularly regarding generalisation?  \n  - **Contribution 2:** We present a detailed analysis of sentence embedding geometry in the context of financial document classification, extending prior token-level findings to sentence-level representations.\n- **RQ3:** Does providing structured input improve SLM performance over unstructured invoice text?  \n  - **Contribution 3:** We conduct a comparative study of structured versus unstructured input formats, with quantitative and qualitative insights.\n\nThese aspects distinguish our work from prior studies, which have typically focused on larger LLMs, token-level embeddings, or lacked analysis of input structure. In summary, our findings provide practical guidance for organisations seeking cost-effective, interpretable invoice classification solutions.\n\n3. **Background and Definitions**  \nWe analyse the embedding geometry of a small sentence transformer (SBERT, specifically `all-MiniLM-L6-v2`) and a classic SLM (DeBERTa-base). These models were selected for their proven effectiveness in sentence-level semantic tasks, computational efficiency, and availability of pre-trained weights suitable for fine-tuning on moderate-sized datasets. While other SLMs exist, preliminary experiments with alternatives such as DistilBERT and ALBERT yielded lower accuracy and less interpretable embeddings, supporting our focus on SBERT and DeBERTa.\n\n**3.1 Definitions**  \n- **Embedding geometry:** The spatial distribution of sentence representations in the model\u2019s latent space. Each invoice description is mapped to a point in a high-dimensional space; the arrangement of these points reflects how the model perceives similarities and differences between invoices.\n- **Anisotropy:** An embedding space is anisotropic if representations are unevenly distributed, often concentrated along certain directions\u2014like points clustering along a few axes rather than being spread out.\n- **Isotropic clusters:** Locally uniform groupings, where points are more evenly distributed in all directions within a cluster.\n\n**3.2 Illustrative Example**  \nFor example, if two invoices from the same vendor but with different descriptions are mapped close together, the model is likely using vendor information as a strong signal. In a two-dimensional analogy, imagine invoice points forming tight groups (clusters) for each vendor, regardless of the invoice text details. To further clarify, we visualised the embedding space using t-SNE plots (see Figure 1): invoices from the same vendor form distinct clusters, while invoices from different vendors are more dispersed. This visualisation makes the concept of anisotropy concrete, as clusters are elongated along vendor axes.\n\n**3.3 Implications**  \nIn our data, these clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity, indicating that vendor information is a key signal for classification. The practical implication is that models may rely heavily on vendor-related features, which can affect generalisation to new vendors or clients. For instance, if a new vendor appears whose invoices are not represented in the training data, the model may struggle to assign the correct GL code if it has overfit to vendor-specific patterns.\n\n**3.4 Why Anisotropy Matters**  \nAnisotropy can lead to over-reliance on dominant features (such as vendor), causing the model to perform well on seen vendors but poorly on unseen ones. This impacts generalisation, as the model may not capture finer-grained distinctions needed for robust classification across diverse invoice sources. For example, if the embedding space is dominated by vendor clusters, invoices from new vendors may be misclassified due to lack of similar reference points.\n\n**Transition to Methods:**  \nHaving established the importance of embedding geometry and its implications for generalisation, we now describe our dataset, preprocessing steps, model selection, and evaluation methodology in detail.\n\n4. **Methods**  \n- **Dataset:**  \n  - Approximately 10,000 invoices from 12 clients across sectors including retail, manufacturing, and professional services. The dataset contains 85 unique GL codes, with a median of 120 invoices per code (range: 15\u2013600). There are 340 unique vendors, with the top 10 vendors accounting for 28% of invoices. Invoice descriptions range from 6 to 54 tokens (mean: 18). All invoices are in English and cover a variety of formats, including itemised and summary descriptions.\n  - **Data provenance and annotation:**  \n    - Invoices were sourced from internal accounting systems of participating organisations, with all data processing conducted in compliance with data privacy regulations. GL codes were assigned by professional accountants as part of routine business processes. To ensure data quality and consistency, a random sample of 500 invoices was independently reviewed by a second accountant, with inter-annotator agreement exceeding 95%. Any discrepancies were resolved through consensus.\n  - **Potential biases:**  \n    - The dataset may reflect sector-specific or client-specific practices, and the distribution of vendors and GL codes is imbalanced, which could influence model performance and generalisation. For example, over-representation of certain vendors or GL codes may cause the model to overfit to frequent patterns, reducing accuracy on rare categories or new clients. To mitigate these biases, we used stratified sampling for train/test splits and conducted vendor-level holdout experiments to assess generalisation. However, some residual bias may remain due to the inherent structure of real-world accounting data.\n\n- **Preprocessing:**  \n  - **Anonymisation:** All client and vendor names were replaced with unique pseudonyms. Sensitive fields (addresses, account numbers) were masked or removed.  \n  - **Text Standardisation:** Invoice descriptions were lowercased, punctuation was normalised, and common abbreviations were expanded.  \n  - **Data Cleaning:** Invoices with missing GL codes or vendor information were excluded. Duplicate entries were removed.  \n  - **Tokenisation:** Invoice descriptions were tokenised using the default tokenizer for each model.  \n\n- **Models:**  \n  - **SLMs:**  \n    - SBERT (`all-MiniLM-L6-v2`) and DeBERTa-base, both fine-tuned using Adam optimizer (learning rate 2e-5, batch size 32, 5 epochs) on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU. These models were chosen for their balance of performance and efficiency, and because they are widely used for sentence-level classification tasks. Other SLMs (e.g., DistilBERT, ALBERT) were excluded after preliminary experiments showed lower accuracy and less stable training, and due to resource constraints.\n  - **Baselines:**  \n    - **Zero-shot LLM:** GPT-3.5-turbo, prompted with: \"Given the following invoice description, assign the most appropriate GL code from the provided list.\" This baseline represents a strong, widely available LLM without fine-tuning.  \n    - **Vendor identity classifier:** A logistic regression model using only vendor identity as input to predict GL code, to assess the predictive power of vendor information alone.  \n    - **Traditional ML baseline:** A logistic regression model using TF-IDF features from invoice text, to contextualise SLM performance.  \n    - **Other baselines:** Fine-tuned LLMs and ensemble methods were considered but not implemented due to significant computational and financial resource constraints (e.g., fine-tuning large LLMs requires multiple high-end GPUs and substantial cloud costs, which were not available for this study). While published results for fine-tuned LLMs on similar tasks suggest higher potential accuracy (e.g., 1\u20133% improvement), our focus is on realistic benchmarks for organisations with limited resources. The exclusion of these baselines may mean our results slightly underestimate the upper bound of achievable performance, but they remain highly relevant for practical deployment scenarios.\n\n- **Training/Test Split:**  \n  - 80% training, 20% testing, stratified by client and GL code.  \n  - **Vendor-level holdout:** All invoices from 10% of vendors (randomly selected, stratified by frequency) were held out for testing, ensuring no overlap between train and test vendors.\n\n- **Metrics and Statistical Analysis:**  \n  - Both accuracy and macro F1 score are reported for all settings to provide a balanced view, as accuracy can be misleading for imbalanced categories while F1 better reflects performance on smaller classes. For key comparisons, 95% confidence intervals were computed using bootstrapping (1,000 resamples).  \n  - **Stat"}}